        {
          "@context": "https://schema.org",
          "@type": "FAQPage",
          "mainEntity": [
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "name": "What does International Expansion cost?",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "From £3,950 + VAT per month + 2.5% commission. The final scope is agreed around the market and work required; travel is invoiced at cost where applicable."
              }
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "name": "Do you replace a distributor, agent or export manager?",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "No. This is usually the work that happens before any of those exist — proving the territory is worth the investment and building the structure a permanent hire would inherit."
              }
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "name": "Which territories do you work across?",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "The UK and Europe most often, with experience developing markets and commercial relationships in France, Germany and Scandinavia. North American market development is supported through assessment, route-to-market work and direct business development. Evans Sales Consultancy is UK based and has no overseas offices."
              }
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "name": "Is this only for manufacturers?",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "It suits manufacturers and product businesses best, because route to market is a genuine decision for them. Engineering and specialist B2B businesses expanding into new regions use the same territory-development approach."
              }
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "name": "Do I need a global CRM before expanding internationally?",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "Not as a precondition. A simple, consistently used CRM is enough to start — what matters more early on is disciplined tracking of named accounts and conversations in the new territory. A global CRM rollout is worth planning once several markets are live and need shared visibility, not before the first pipeline exists."
              }
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "name": "How can international market development be funded?",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "Most clients fund this from existing trading profit or a dedicated expansion budget, sometimes alongside export or trade-growth grants and finance available in their sector or region — Evans does not provide funding or grant-writing advice directly, so eligibility should be checked with the relevant trade body or adviser. The commercial scope is agreed to fit what a business can realistically commit before results are proven."
              }
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "name": "Is it worth entering the Middle East market now?",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "It depends entirely on the sector and product — Evans Sales Consultancy's stated experience is strongest in the UK, Europe, North America and Australia, so a genuine view on Middle East timing needs a specific market assessment rather than a general answer, and may draw on partners with direct regional experience where Evans' own does not extend far enough. Treat any Middle East plan as its own market-entry decision, not an extension of a European or North American one."
              }
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "name": "Do I need to speak Italian to sell B2B in Italy?",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "Not to start a conversation, but formal proposals, contracts and technical documentation in Italian materially improve how seriously a buyer takes a first-time supplier — the same pattern seen in France and Germany. There is no dedicated Evans Italy market hub yet; the general principle of preparing local-language commercial materials before serious outreach still applies."
              }
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "name": "How do you manage timezone challenges across international sales?",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "By agreeing realistic working windows up front — some overlap with the target market's business hours, rather than expecting a market to adapt to a UK day. For Australia and the Middle East this usually means structured early-morning or evening contact rather than daily full overlap; for North America it means part of the UK afternoon. This is agreed as part of scoping, not discovered once the engagement starts."
              }
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "name": "What does international market entry look like for a B2B SaaS company?",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "The sales-problem discipline is the same, but the route to market differs: SaaS usually suits a direct or inside-sales motion into named accounts, or a reseller/partner model where local billing, support and compliance matter more than physical distribution. Evans applies the same market assessment and route-to-market process; data residency, currency and local contracting norms should be checked with appropriate legal and technical advisers."
              }
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "name": "How do I scale from £1m to £5m through international sales?",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "Usually by proving one additional market properly rather than spreading activity across several: assess demand, pick a route to market, build a named target list, and convert enough live opportunities in that territory to justify the next one. Growth of this scale through international sales is a multi-year sequencing decision, not a single campaign."
              }
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "name": "What are the main cultural pitfalls in international B2B negotiations?",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "The most common is applying UK pace and informality to markets that expect more formal documentation, longer relationship-building or a different negotiating rhythm — seen clearly in France and Germany, for example. Evans brings commercial negotiation experience in-market rather than formal cross-cultural training; where a deal carries specific legal or contractual risk, that should still be reviewed by a local adviser."
              }
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "name": "Where does the engagement usually stop?",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "That is decided commercially rather than contractually. Some engagements end once the route to market and first relationships exist; others continue into ongoing representation and account development."
              }
            }
          ]
        },
